            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

During the blower door test, we had identified some remaining air leakage &amp; heat loss locations:



** The main ones are associated with the intentional openings for the current furnace - the 5" chimney &amp; the 6" open combustion air supply. In addition, the 5" chimney passes into the concrete block chimney structure that is not well sealed and has air leakage through it. If you were to upgrade to an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified high-efficiency condensing gas furnace ........... you would eliminate all 3 of these air leakage locations !!



** The attic hatch does not seal well and can be sealed with an "easily removable" caulking, such as Draft Attack.



** The perimeter of the header space in the basement still has some air leakage and could be better air sealed if you removed the existing insulation and spray foamed this space. Or .......... some folks will leave the current insulation in there &amp; cut pieces of rigid board insulation to install in each header space and spray foam it around the perimeter.



Savings: I would expect you can achieve a 30% reduction in air leakage, and the savings of this are integrated in with the heating system upgrade section of this report.</airSeal>
                <mainWalls />
                <ceilings />
                <ceilingsCathedral />
                <exposedFloors />
                <foundation />
                <windows />
                <doors />
                <VentilationSystem>

If you proceed with the air sealing section of this report, this will remove much of the open air leakage (heat loss) that naturally occurs in this house.



We identified during my visit that the new kitchen range hood fan was installed with a vent pipe up through the chimney &amp; out the roof, however it appears the vent plate in the range hood was never removed to allow it to vent that way - instead, it vents back inside the kitchen. This range hood has a 300 CFM capacity and you may find that as you seal up the other leakage locations, it will become more important that your "intentional" exhaust fans operate as they were expected to.

</VentilationSystem>
                <HotWaterSystem>

Your house currently has a 182 litre electric hot water tank. If you decide to upgrade your furnace to a high efficiency, direct-vent propane model, you should consider replacing the hot water tank with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an energy factor of 0.92 or higher.



Savings: By replacing your current hot water tank with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.92, could potentially save $ 1,144 per year in energy costs and qualify for up to $ 750 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

 

To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your house is currently heated with an 84.4% efficient oil furnace, that has a 5" chimney (vented into &amp; up an older cement block chimney) and it also has an "open" 6" outside combustion air supply.



I realize this furnace is new, however I want to highlight the estimated energy &amp; cost savings (referenced in my previous report) if you were to replace this with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified high-efficiency condensing gas furnace with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE, including a high efficiency ECM motor to circulate the heat.



Savings: By replacing your current furnace with a 95% AFUE high-efficiency condensing gas furnace, that incorporates an ECM fan blower, you could potentially save $ 1,499 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 31.0 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 1,500 in rebates from the Energy Efficiency Incentive Program (EEIP).

 

To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</HeatingSystem>
                <Renewables />
                <WaterConservation />
                <AdditionalComments>

LED Lights



Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to flourescent lighting. Floursecent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And ..... LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.



Rebates: In Yellowknife, LED light bulbs, tubes and integrated fixtures are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 200 (per fiscal year)  from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (the kitchen range hood not currently venting outside, 1 bathroom fan and the clothes dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 1.8 Pascals. As this number is lower than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment is not expected to cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current furnace vents up a chimney and could allow combustion gases back-drafted into the house if the depressurization exceeded 5 Pascals.



During my assessment of your home, there were both smoke and carbon monoxide (CO) detectors. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Ventilation



Your home currently has the following ventilation:

        - A kitchen range hood (not currently venting outside but you indicated it is supposed to)

        - A bathroom fan

        - The clothes dryer



If you proceed with the air sealing section of this report, this will remove much of the open air leakage (heat loss) that naturally occurs in this house.



We identified during my visit that the new kitchen range hood fan was installed with a vent pipe up through the chimney &amp; out the roof, however it appears the vent plate in the range hood was never removed to allow it to vent that way - instead, it vents back inside the kitchen. This range hood has a 300 CFM capacity and you may find that as you seal up the other leakage locations, it will become more important that your "intentional" exhaust fans operate as they were expected to.



Many homes now have timer switches connected to bathroom fans (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so these fans can remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odours without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs - Yellowknife



The following Nov. 1, 2019 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:

Electricity - $19.04 min, $0.33 per kWh

Oil  - $1.21 per litre 

Propane - $0.68 per litre

Wood - $420.00 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $5.51 per bag



